// pfodWeb NOTE: GPIO0 tagged "button" - this board is iarduino.ru's
// "Metro ESP-32" controller (added to arduino-esp32 by PR #3542, "Added
// Piranha ESP-32 and Metro ESP-32 boards", drondistortion, Jan 2020).
// Its sibling ESP32 product from the same vendor, the Piranha ESP32 v2,
// is documented on iarduino's own wiki as having an onboard BOOT
// pushbutton wired to the GPIO0 strapping pin
// (wiki.iarduino.ru/page/piranha-es32-wiki/: "Кнопка BOOT служит для
// ручного перевода модуля в режим прошивки"). GPIO0 is also the
// universal boot-mode strapping pin used by a pushbutton on essentially
// every ESP32 dev board. Treated as a confirmed onboard user/boot
// button; capabilities restricted to digital_input+button only.
//
// pfodWeb NOTE: GPIO12 excluded from chipGpios entirely (and its
// pins_arduino.h alias removed - see that file's own note). iarduino's
// "Metro" product line (this board plus the earlier ESP8266-based
// "Metro ESP") ships a 5-pin OUT connector for daisy-chaining the
// vendor's proprietary "Metro module" I2C accessories: SDA, SCL, an
// address-management line, 5V and GND (wiki.iarduino.ru/page/metro-esp/
// documents this exact connector/pin layout on the sibling ESP8266
// board, there wired to a different GPIO). The iarduino_Metro Arduino
// library's module-detection/address-assignment function takes an
// address-line pin argument that defaults to pin D12 on this board
// family (per forum.arduino.ru/t/esp32-gpio12-input-mode/19470,
// discussing the iarduino_Metro library), matching this board's own
// header exactly. This GPIO is committed to onboard module-chain
// address management, not general-purpose I/O.
